WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
So today I wanted to talk about three ways that you can ingest a CSV into Positron.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.060
Some people call this importing, some people call this loading.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.560
In any case, it's bringing in a CSV into your environment so then you can actually

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.600
work with the data and do some analysis.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.360
Here I have Positron open.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.240
I'm going to create a new folder through this welcome screen.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.700
I'll call it Ingest CSVs.

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:32.340
I open up that folder and it's going to prompt me and ask if I want to create a Python environment.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.980
Positron does this using a tool called uv and I'm going to hit yes.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.820
In the file explorer, you notice that there is a new virtual environment that has been

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.080
opened up.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.099
From here I can actually install some packages.

00:00:46.099 --> 00:00:52.340
I'm going to open up my terminal and I'm going to run the uv command uv pip install

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:53.340
pandas.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:59.380
Pandas is a very common Python library for manipulating data.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.860
And I'm also going to run uv pip install polars.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:09.500
Polars is a relatively newer tool for data manipulation in Python.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:15.739
Here I have my CSV in my downloads folder and you can actually just drag and drop your

00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:19.419
CSV directly into your file explorer.

00:01:19.419 --> 00:01:23.739
Now it's popped up here, so that means it's available within the folder.

00:01:23.739 --> 00:01:28.739
Positron automatically shows me a preview of the data using the data explorer.

00:01:28.739 --> 00:01:33.419
So I can take a quick view of what's contained in the CSV.

00:01:33.419 --> 00:01:37.620
But if you look at the variables pane, you'll notice that it's not actually loaded into

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.139
my session.

00:01:39.139 --> 00:01:41.019
This is just a preview.

00:01:41.019 --> 00:01:46.019
So if I actually want to load the CSV, here are the three ways that we could do that.

00:01:46.019 --> 00:01:51.260
So the first one is I'm going to hit command shift P to open up the command palette and

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.220
I'm going to select Python, new Python file.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:02:00.180
And so of course the first way that we can import our data is writing a script.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.699
From here I can write import polars as PL.

00:02:04.699 --> 00:02:11.220
I run that line to import polars and then I could do something like dad equals PL dot

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.660
read CSV, then the name of the file.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.059
And running that line, I can see that in my variables pane, the CSV is now loaded into

00:02:20.059 --> 00:02:21.660
my session.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.960
The second way I want to show you is through the import data button in Positron.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.100
If I click this button and select my CSV, it can actually provide the pandas or the

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:36.020
R code for importing that data for reproducibility.

00:02:36.059 --> 00:02:44.860
But if I hit import, it also directly imports that CSV into my session.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:49.460
And the last way I want to show you that you can import data in Positron is you can use

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:50.699
AI.

00:02:50.699 --> 00:02:57.539
So I'm going to open up Posit Assistant and then I can actually attach a file by clicking

00:02:57.539 --> 00:03:01.660
the file button and I'll select my CSV.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:07.059
And then I can tell Posit Assistant something like import this data set.

00:03:07.059 --> 00:03:10.699
Just to do something different, I'm going to say using R because in Positron we can

00:03:10.699 --> 00:03:13.339
switch between Python and R.

00:03:13.339 --> 00:03:15.339
And so it's going to go ahead and do that.

00:03:15.339 --> 00:03:19.300
I'll provide the R code for importing the CSV.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:23.880
And now instead of our Python environment, we're actually in an R environment and we

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:29.059
have this data set, the CSV imported as a data frame.

00:03:29.059 --> 00:03:32.699
We hope that you try importing some data into Positron and let us know how it goes.

